      "provenance": {
        "notes": "NAME CLASH: in the UK and Spain 'geranium' usually means Pelargonium; the hardy cranesbill (Geranium) is a different plant and a different row. NC State files this under Pelargonium x hybridum (previously x hortorum); RHS uses x hortorum (name status 'Unresolved'), so the two pages are treated as the same plant. Watering: RHS says let the compost dry between waterings, NC State says container plants can dry between waterings; mapped to let_top_dry (agent). Light: NC State full sun (6+ hours), low light gives leggy plants; RHS full sun or partial shade; stored as the sunnier reading. Size: RHS 0.1 to 0.5 m for both height and spread, NC State up to about 90 cm tall (1 to 3 ft) with no spread; RHS figures stored (pot plant). Tolerances and rest factor: no page gives numbers (both say little water in winter). Temperature: pages give overwintering minimums and hardiness classes (H2) but no indoor range. Toxicity: only NC State states it (low severity, leaves, geraniol and linalool; vomiting and appetite loss, cats most sensitive, mild contact dermatitis, a problem for cats, dogs, horses and people); RHS is silent. The grade 1 is the agent's mapping from 'low'. The ASPCA page was not read for this row. The NC State genus page was also read and agrees."
      },
